// Mutating objects in place can introduce unintended side effects and hidden
// dependencies, making application behavior harder to reason about, test, and debug.
//
// This creates several problems:
//
// - Shared-state side effects: Modifying an object can unexpectedly affect
//   other code holding a reference to it.
// - Reduced predictability: Functions may change their inputs rather than
//   produce new outputs, obscuring data flow.
// - Harder debugging: The original state is lost, making it difficult to
//   identify where and why values changed.
// - Broken referential equality: Frameworks, memoization, and change-detection
//   mechanisms may fail to recognize updates.
// - Reduced composability: Functions that mutate shared objects are harder to
//   reuse, test independently, and execute safely.
//
// Recommendation: Prefer constructing and returning new objects using spread
// syntax or immutable transformations. Keep necessary mutation within clearly
// defined ownership boundaries, such as adapters managing external APIs.
//
// Scope clarification: This rule detects direct property assignments and
// `Object.assign()` mutations, with specific exceptions. It does not prohibit
// all forms of object mutation or guarantee immutability.
//
// Principle: Transform values rather than modifying existing state.
//
// Three patterns are deliberately absent because something else already owns
// them. Computed assignment `obj[key] = value` is reported by the array rule,
// whose index pattern matches it; `delete obj.prop` is reported by Biome's
// own noDelete; and a nested assignment `obj.a.b = 1` already matches the
// plain property pattern, with the member expression bound as the object.
//
// Map and Set mutation is not covered. `.set` and `.add` are ordinary method
// names, and flagging every call would report builders, emitters and request
// objects that have nothing to do with collections.
//
// Mutating a locally built object that never escapes is harmless, and this
// rule reports it anyway: GritQL matches syntax, not lifetimes. That is the
// conservative trade — a visible false positive over a silent gap.

language js

or {
  `$obj.$prop += $val` as $stmt where {
    $obj <: not "this",
    $prop <: not or {
      "current", "displayName",
      "style", "dataset", "innerHTML", "innerText", "textContent",
      "className", "src", "href", "scrollTop", "scrollLeft",
      "width", "height", "value", "checked", "disabled", "title",
      "locals", "statusCode",
      "length"
    },
    register_diagnostic(
      span = $stmt,
      message = "`+=` reads a property and writes it back. Build the new value and return it rather than editing the one you were given.",
      severity = "error"
    )
  },
  `$obj.$prop -= $val` as $stmt where {
    $obj <: not "this",
    $prop <: not or {
